* [2026-07-01 Wed]

[13:58]

I have been looking at different ways to include logic into the
training of language models (preferably small).


In my work I have observed that when we work with AI and language we
often end up in a situation where we have good ideas and can find
algorithms that can help us solve different problems in
categorisation, information extraction, knowledge management, etc. We
have a lot of data and knowledge about the data. The issue is that we
do not have a lot of training data and no good ways of generating
training data. The subject matter experts (SMEs) do not have the time
or patience to annotate data. The annotated data often has low quality
from a machine learning perspective as the SMEs get bored and/or do
not understand how simple the task we are asking them to do is as they
expect the AI system to be smart.

What we would like to do is to be able to convert the knowledge about
the data into logic that we can use in training and inference.

This is why I have been investigating neurosymbolic approaches.

Some of the key topics I have looked at are:
 - Provenance semirings
 - Datalog
 - Differentiability
 - Combinatorial exploration
 - Probabilistic computation
 - Recursion
 - Scalability

I have found the scallop-lang project. A programming language for
working with neursymbolic computing. It is based on datalog and can
interact with python.

Another project is dolphin. It is a python package that uses
"provenance semirings" to translate probabilities to symbolic
execution with pytorch.

As training time, computation, and scale is important, I tihnk the
dolphin approach is closer to the right thing. I am thinking about how
to combine it with prolog and constraint logic programming for finite
domains.

I also want to investigate how *xgrammar* handles the symbolic part of
introducing the grammar.

The first result from the project is an accepted pull request to
scallop. A project built for neurosymbolic programming. I updated some
of the dependencies. And made it compatible with python 3.14.

I belive I want to do something similar to what dolphin is doing. I am
not convinced it is "THE" way, but now I need to get a model running
so that we can start to see what it would look like to get the model
included in the project.


Keywords for investigating later:
    - xgrammar
    - constraint logic programming
    - tropical semirings
    - probabilistic programming
    - pyro
    - Executing datalog with pytorch?


